messages:
Maya: FYI, we've got an escalated customer (internal ticket ACME-1289) reporting that when they use streaming function/tool-calls the 'arguments' JSON is getting truncated mid-stream. Their client-side parser fails with a JSONError. Pasting the rough stream capture they provided below.

Maya: (customer stream dump)
```
data: {
data:   "type": "tool_call",
data:   "name": "createIssue",
data:   "arguments": {
data:     "title": "Crash on signup",
data:     "description": "Steps to reproduce:\n1) click signup\n2) crash occurs when password is emoji",
data:     "metadata": {
data:       "user_id": 9876,
data:       "env": "staging"
data:     }
data:   }
data: }
data: [TRUNCATED]
```

Maya: The last chunk above ends with "[TRUNCATED]" in their capture; the client is expecting one JSON blob per tool_call but sometimes receives an incomplete last chunk and tries to parse early. They say it happens ~1-2% of streams, higher for very long argument payloads (large multiline description).

Lucas: Thanks, adding notes. Do we know which SDK/version they used?

Maya: They are on our Node SDK v0.8.3 using streaming via the http fetch wrapper. Also tested raw curl stream, they saw the same truncated chunk.

Ivy: Quick hypothesis: two possible classes of causes - (A) model stops generating mid-JSON (reason: temp stop/token limit) and we send an incomplete JSON as final stream chunk; (B) networking/proxy/chunking where a chunk boundary happens and their client tries to parse before the stream has signaled EOF or final sentinel.

Rachel: Customer reports their parser expects the tool-call arguments to be emitted as one valid JSON object in the stream. If the model emits partial JSON and then continues, the client should buffer until it sees a complete object. We need to clarify what the streaming contract guarantees.

Maya: Repro steps they provided (we validated):
1) Create a function/tool that expects a single large JSON object in arguments (multiline text + arrays).
2) Call the /v1/generate endpoint with stream=true and function_call:auto (or SDK default).
3) Consume stream as server-sent events / incremental chunks. They showed code like below where the client attempts to JSON.parse on each chunk:
```js
// naive client pattern (bad)
fetch(url, { method: 'POST', body: payload, headers })
  .then(res => res.body.getReader())
  .then(async (r) => {
    while (true) {
      const { value, done } = await r.read();
      if (done) break;
      const chunk = new TextDecoder().decode(value);
      // BAD: assumes each chunk is a complete JSON object
      try {
        const parsed = JSON.parse(chunk);
        // handle tool_call
      } catch (e) {
        console.error('parse error', e);
      }
    }
  });
```

Maya: They said shifting to a buffered parser (accumulate chunks until braces match / until final `}` or until a sentinel) fixed their client 100% in our testing. But that's a client-side workaround. We need to confirm the server-side guarantees and see if we can add stronger framing / sentinel for tool calls so naive clients don't break.

Lucas: Agree we should document that streaming is chunked and that chunk boundaries are arbitrary; clients must reassemble. But we should also make streaming tool_call easier to consume. Possible mitigations: (1) emit a small sentinel event/marker after the full tool_call (e.g., a dedicated data: event with {"tool_call_complete":true}) so clients that handle line-by-line can detect completion; (2) ensure the runtime tries to emit the tool_call 'arguments' as a single contiguous chunk when below a threshold; (3) update SDK to buffer partial bytes and expose higher-level events (onToolCall) that deliver fully assembled arguments.

Ivy: For an immediate customer-facing mitigation we can suggest two quick things to the customer: (A) update client to accumulate until balanced braces or until the stream sends its completion event; (B) use non-streaming call to get the full JSON in one response (if latency isn't critical). We can also offer to roll a small SDK patch that reconstructs tool_call args for Node SDK consumers.

Maya: I tested with curl --no-buffer and saw the same fragmentation, which suggests it's not the SDK only. Repro curl:
```bash
curl -N -H 'Authorization: Bearer <KEY>' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"model":"open-qa","input":"...","stream":true,"function_call":"auto"}' \
  https://api.redwood-inference.example/v1/generate
```

Maya: Customer attached request-id: req-20240301-9fc2a (we matched logs). Time window: 2024-03-01T14:23:40Z. Model: rwl-4x-large (they said).

Lucas: I pulled logs for req-20240301-9fc2a. Runtime saw model generate 6 tokens that began a JSON object but the generator stopped mid-object due to the model hitting the configured max_new_tokens of 256; our stream delivered whatever tokens the model produced and then closed. So cause A (model stops mid-JSON) confirmed for that request. We should validate that we always emit all tokens up to generation end and then the stream is properly closed with the final chunk. If the model stops mid-JSON and that's the final generation, the client will indeed receive incomplete JSON.

Ivy: Two problem statements: 1) If generation ends mid-JSON, the underlying behavior is expected (model decided to stop). Should we post-process generated tool_call arguments to check for JSON validity and, if truncated, either attempt to continue generation (auto-resume) or emit a deterministic failure/result to the client? 2) Client expectations vs contract: clarify in docs that models can stop arbitrarily.

Maya: Customer is pushing for server-side help because they can't change all clients quickly. They want either a server completion sentinel or an auto-retry that attempts to continue generation until valid JSON is produced.

Lucas: Auto-resume is tricky: continuing generation may change the semantics or repeat tokens. But we can implement a safer option: if a tool_call arguments JSON is syntactically invalid at generator finish, we attempt up to one quick continuation with a short max_new_tokens=64 and a high temperature=0 to coax the model to finish the JSON (and tag that as post-processing). That would be an opt-in runtime feature (and visible in logs). Would need product signoff and we must surface altered provenance.

Ivy: For short term, I can push an SDK patch for Node that exposes a higher-level 'onToolCall' event that only fires when it receives a complete syntactic JSON (buffers chunks and checks for balanced braces). That should patch most naive clients without changing server behavior. We can also add a small doc section advising robust streaming parsing.

Rachel: Customer asks for timeline. Can we promise a patch within two weeks?

Lucas: SDK patch -> yes, we can ship in ~3 business days as a minor release if prioritized. Runtime auto-resume -> needs design + product approval, estimate 2-4 sprints. Docs update -> 1 day.

Maya: Action items:
- Maya: Send interim mitigation to customer (buffer client, or non-streaming).
- Lucas: Draft SDK change for Node streaming parser (buffer+onToolCall) and open PR.
- Ivy: Draft product note about auto-resume options and risks.
- Rachel: Notify customer success and set expectation for 3-day SDK patch.

deploy-bot: Deploy queued: node-sdk/0.8.4 patch-buf-parser by Lucas scheduled to rollout to staging in ~30m.

Lucas: PR opened: github.com/redwood-inference/node-sdk/pull/342 - adds StreamToolCallAssembler and onToolCall event. Tests included. Will request review.

Maya: I'll attach the customer's truncated stream capture and the replay logs to the PR. Also adding the internal ticket ACME-1289 to the PR description.

Ivy: Long-term note for eng-runtime: think about a server-side framing/event that marks 'tool_call_complete' so clients doing naive per-chunk parsing can at least get a final clear signal. Let's discuss in the runtime planning meeting next Wed.

Rachel: I'll update the customer with the 3-day SDK patch ETA and the recommended temporary workaround.

Maya: Thanks all, marking this thread resolved for now but leaving for follow-ups on the PR and runtime design.
